                            <span class="checkbox-group-description" style="max-width: 60%;">A Multi-Layer Perceptron
                                (MLP) is a type of neural network that learns to make predictions by adjusting the
                                strength of connections between layers of neurons. It consists of one or more hidden
                                layers, each with a defined number of neurons (<b>hidden layer sizes</b>), which allows
                                the model to capture complex patterns in the data. During training, the model updates
                                these connections using an optimization algorithm (<b>solver</b>), such as adam or sgd,
                                over a set number of iterations (<b>epochs</b>). The learning process is influenced by
                                the <b>initial learning rate</b>, which determines how quickly the model adjusts with
                                each step. This rate can be constant or adapt over time (<b>learning rate schedule</b>).
                                Each neuron in the network uses an <b>activation function</b> (like ReLU or logistic) to
                                decide how to process inputs, helping the model handle non-linear relationships. To
                                prevent overfitting, a regularization term (<b>alpha</b>) can be applied, encouraging
                                the model to prefer simpler patterns. Together, these elements define how the MLP learns
                                and generalizes from data.</span>
