                        <div id="rf-group" class="checkbox-group" style="display: none; text-align: left;">
                            <span class="checkbox-group-description" style="max-width: 60%;">Random Forest is an
                                ensemble algorithm that builds
                                multiple decision trees and combines their predictions to make a more robust and
                                accurate final prediction. The model consists of a collection of trees (<b>number of
                                    estimators</b>), where each tree contributes a vote to the final decision. To
                                control the complexity of each individual tree, the <b>maximum depth</b> is defined,
                                which limits how deep each tree can grow, preventing overfitting. During the
                                construction of each tree, only a random subset of features is considered for each split
                                (<b>max features</b>), increasing diversity among the trees. The <b>criterion</b>
                                determines the metric used to evaluate the quality of each split. To ensure that splits
                                are statistically meaningful, the <b>minimum samples per leaf</b> is established,
                                defining the minimum number of samples each leaf must contain, and the <b>minimun
                                    samples per split</b> specifies how many samples are required for an internal node
                                to be split. These hyperparameters work together to balance the model's ability to
                                capture complex patterns while maintaining good generalization capability.</span>
                        </div>
